+### Sessions, and the candidates they mint
+
+A session records the identity it was admitted under in its own meta
+(`.autosk/sessions/<id>.json`, key `runtime_identity`) — the same record as the
+task's pin, written by the same canonicaliser and read back by the same
+validation, so a corrupt one is refused rather than repaired into a plausible
+one. A session runs the workflow object it captured at dispatch, so this is the
+durable answer to *which code produced this transcript* after the process that
+ran it is gone.
+
+That capture is also what a **candidate** is checked against. A workflow fans out
+by creating tasks and enrolling them — a panel seat, an Arena candidate, a
+code-review child. Enrolling enters a workflow at its first step, which is a
+restart and therefore pins whatever is registered *now*. For an operator that is
+right. For a session it is not: an `ext update` between the session's dispatch and
+its `task create --workflow` would let the Epic fan out from code it is no longer
+admitted to, with nothing recording it. So the per-session MCP path re-asks
+whether **the session itself is still admitted**, and refuses the new task with
+`extension_version_mismatch` when it is not.
+
+The question is about the parent, not the child. Requiring the candidate's
+workflow to come from the parent's own distribution would be a different rule, and
+a wrong one: a registry holds one distribution per package, per extension
+subdirectory and per bare file, so fanning out into a workflow from another
+package is the ordinary shape — the extensions shipped here *are* separate
+packages. A candidate enters on its own distribution exactly as an operator
+enrolling it would, with its own pin — including when that distribution is the
+one that was just updated, because the candidate is a new task that was never
+admitted to anything and the Epic's own code is unchanged.
+
+The refused create leaves the task behind, unenrolled and unpinned, because the
+create and the enrol are two steps and only the second is refused. That is
+visible rather than silent — the same residue any rejected enrol leaves, an
+unknown workflow included — and nothing about it is admitted anywhere.
+
+Two enrolment paths are deliberately outside this. The `task.enroll` RPC has no
+session behind it — it is the operator surface, and re-pinning on a first-step
+enroll is exactly how a task parked by an update is re-admitted. An interactive
+(taskless) chat entered no workflow, so it has no admission to pass on. An agent
+that shells out to the `autosk` CLI reaches the RPC surface too, and the daemon
+cannot attribute that call to the session it came from; that is a limit of this
+check, not something it covers.
+
